#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373d4ad5-ef39-5f03-bba4-d9be901e2587" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "d496f23e-6ef7-5ec2-bb6c-054759a5b033" :question/math-academy-id "e-1327" :question/problem "Factor $x^2 + 12x + 36$." :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 = (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\nx^2 + 12x + 36\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 12x + 6^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 6x + 6^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{6}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(x + 6)^{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "457399ac-1ede-551f-968b-9ef270a67d0b" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"}] :question/id #uuid "29cbd59e-9eff-5439-9551-4520f68707b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1328" :question/problem "Factor $p^2 - 10p + 25$." :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\np^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} p + a^2 = (p-{\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\np^2 - 10p + 25\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\np^{2} - 10p + 5^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\np^{2} - 2 \\cdot 5p + 5^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(p - 5)^{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48f257af-1f00-5cf9-89cd-3d44d21482ec" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial with Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "2fb03f0f-25c2-57a6-9e33-da837af2f339" :question/math-academy-id "e-1329" :question/problem "Factor the expression $y^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}$." :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 = (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\ny^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}y + (\\frac{1}{5})^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\ny^{2} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y + (\\frac{1}{5})^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n(y + \\frac{1}{5})^{2}\n$$"}]}
